@@ -407,6 +407,15 @@ sections:
           `.["foo"]` (`.foo` above is a shorthand version of this, but
           only for identifier-like strings).
 
+          Indexes can be chained, with or without a separating dot:
+          `.["foo"].["bar"]` and `.["foo"]["bar"]` are equivalent to
+          `.foo.bar`.
+
+        examples:
+          - program: '.["media:content"].["media:credits"]'
+            input: '{"media:content":{"media:credits":"Alice"}}'
+            output: ['"Alice"']
+
       - title: "Array Index: `.[<number>]`"
         body: |
 
@@ -417,6 +426,9 @@ sections:
           Negative indices are allowed, with -1 referring to the last
           element, -2 referring to the next to last element, and so on.
 
+          Array indexes can also be chained: `.foo[0]` and `.foo.[0]`
+          are equivalent.
+
         examples:
           - program: '.[0]'
             input: '[{"name":"JSON", "good":true}, {"name":"XML", "good":false}]'
@@ -441,6 +453,9 @@ sections:
           backwards from the end of the array), or omitted (in which
           case it refers to the start or end of the array).
 
+          Slices can also be chained: `.foo[1:3]` and `.foo.[1:3]`
+          are equivalent.
+
         examples:
           - program: '.[2:4]'
             input: '["a","b","c","d","e"]'
@@ -470,6 +485,9 @@ sections:
           You can also use this on an object, and it will return all
           the values of the object.
 
+          Iterators can also be chained: `.foo[]` and `.foo.[]`
+          are equivalent.
+
         examples:
           - program: '.[]'
             input: '[{"name":"JSON", "good":true}, {"name":"XML", "good":false}]'
